    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d95">

                <pb facs="0257" n="125" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg125"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    Leahy Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 95 <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Commanding General, United States Military Mission
                        in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Deane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Commanding General, Twelfth Army Group</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Bradley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-25">25 June 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>To Bradley citing <hi
                        rendition="#small-caps">Shgsd</hi> and to General Marshall for his eyes
                        only from Deane
                    top sec
                    MX 24794.</p>
                <p>As indicated in my M–24786, this date,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d95-f1"/> immediately upon receipt of your S–92697<anchor n="2" xml:id="d95-f2"/> at 2050Z<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d95-f3"/> on the 23rd, I submitted your request to General Antonov. At the same time the
                    Ambassador submitted the same request to Mr. Vyshinski of the Foreign Office<anchor n="4" xml:id="d95-f4"/>
                    who is to have charge of the arrangements in Berlin.</p>
                <p>Sunday, 24 June, there was a victory celebration in Moscow and <pb facs="0258"
                        n="126" xml:id="pg126"/> no work was done. Thus far we have not had a reply
                    to your request despite constant pressure on our part.</p>
                <p>We fully appreciate the importance of starting on the communication installations
                    and will continue to use every means at our disposal to get Soviet approval.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
